.TH PYDEBUPREP 1 "October 2026" "pydebuprep 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pydebuprep \- read\-only health report for Debian and Ubuntu after a release upgrade
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pydebuprep
.RB [ report ]
.RB [ \-\-format\ \fIFORMAT\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-output\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR \ |\  \-\-output\ \fIFILE\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-baseline\ \fIFILE\fR \ |\  \-\-no\-baseline ]
.RB [ \-\-snapshot\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-checks\ \fIID,...\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-skip\ \fIID,...\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-min\-severity\ \fILEVEL\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.RB [ \-\-check\-option\ \fICHECK.OPTION=VALUE\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-config\ \fIFILE\fR \ |\  \-\-no\-config ]
.br
.B pydebuprep convert
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-format\ \fIFORMAT\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-output\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR \ |\  \-\-output\ \fIFILE\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.br
.B pydebuprep snapshot
.RB [ \-\-snapshot\-dir\ \fIDIR\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-check\-option\ \fICHECK.OPTION=VALUE\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-config\ \fIFILE\fR \ |\  \-\-no\-config ]
.br
.B pydebuprep list\-checks
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]
.br
.B pydebuprep configtest
.RB [ \-\-config\ \fIFILE\fR \ |\  \-\-no\-config ]
.br
.B pydebuprep example\-config
.br
.B pydebuprep completion
.BR bash \ |\  zsh
.br
.B pydebuprep
.BR \-\-version \ |\  \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pydebuprep
inspects a Debian (12, 13) or Ubuntu (22.04, 24.04, 26.04) system after a
release upgrade. It reports on APT sources and package state, configuration file
conflicts, the running kernel, DKMS modules and the bootloader, systemd units,
timers and cron jobs, time synchronisation, the journal and kernel log, network
interfaces, routes and DNS, firewall rules, AppArmor, the SSH server configuration,
unattended\-upgrades, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, postfix, BIND, Docker and Python
virtualenvs, fstab and disk space. See
.BR CHECKS .
.PP
It is strictly read\-only. It never installs, removes or reconfigures anything,
never runs
.BR "apt update" ,
and never restarts services. Each finding includes the commands to fix it, for
you to review and run yourself. Every external command it runs is checked
against a built\-in allowlist of read\-only commands, and every release is
traced with strace on each supported release to prove that a run as root
changes nothing.
.PP
Run it as root for complete results. Checks that need root are reported as
skipped otherwise.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B report
Run all checks and print a report. This is the default when no command is
given. If a snapshot exists, the newest one is used as a baseline and each
check reports what changed since it was taken.
.TP
.BI convert " FILE"
Render a report saved earlier with
.B \-\-format json
or
.B \-\-format yaml
in another format, for example to paste a host's report into Jira later.
.I FILE
may be
.B \-
for standard input. The output is the same as running
.B report
in that format at the time; the timestamp and host in file names come from the
saved report. Text, HTML, Markdown, Jira and Confluence reports can't be read
back, so keep a JSON or YAML copy. The file is validated field by field; an
invalid one is rejected with the field at fault and exit status 3. Exits 0 on
success, whatever the report's findings.
.TP
.B snapshot
Record a baseline before upgrading: OS release, kernel, active and enabled
services, timers and cron jobs, APT sources, held and installed packages, mounts,
DKMS modules, network interfaces, routes, DNS and network manager, firewall rules,
the time service, AppArmor profiles, unattended\-upgrades, running Docker
containers and online PostgreSQL clusters. Prints the path of the snapshot file,
which only root can read.
.TP
.B list\-checks
List the available checks with their IDs and categories. With
.BR \-\-verbose ,
also list each check's options and their defaults.
.TP
.B configtest
Read and validate the configuration file, report every problem found, and exit
0 if it is valid or 3 if not. Prints
.B Syntax OK
on success.
.TP
.B example\-config
Print a commented configuration file containing every setting at its default.
.TP
.BR completion " " bash | zsh
Print a shell completion script. The package already installs both; this is
for other installations.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format:
.BR text " (default), " json ", " yaml ", " html ", " markdown ,
.B jira
(Jira wiki markup) or
.B confluence
(Confluence wiki markup, for Insert > Markup > Confluence wiki).
All formats contain the same information in the same order.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Write the report to
.I DIR/pydebuprep\-HOSTNAME\-YYYYmmddTHHMMSSZ.EXT
instead of standard output. The directory must exist.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to
.IR FILE .
If it already exists, a UTC timestamp is inserted before the extension; an
existing file is never overwritten.
.B \-o \-
writes to standard output, even when the configuration sets
.BR output_dir .
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " FILE"
Compare against this snapshot instead of the newest one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-baseline
Do not compare against any snapshot.
.TP
.BI \-\-snapshot\-dir " DIR"
Where snapshots are stored and looked up (default
.IR /var/lib/pydebuprep/snapshots ).
.TP
.BI \-\-checks " ID,..."
Run only the listed checks.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " ID,..."
Do not run the listed checks.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-severity " LEVEL"
Hide findings below
.IR LEVEL :
.BR info " (default), " warning " or " critical .
The exit status still counts hidden findings.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never colorize text output. Color is also disabled when standard output is not
a terminal or the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is set.
.TP
.BI \-\-check\-option " CHECK.OPTION=VALUE"
Set an option of one check, overriding the configuration file. May be given
more than once; repeating a list option adds an item each time. List values are
otherwise split on commas, except for regular\-expression options such as
.BR ignore ,
which take each value whole. Use
.B list\-checks \-\-verbose
to see the options.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read settings from
.I FILE
instead of
.IR /etc/pydebuprep.conf .
The file must exist.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Ignore any configuration file and use built\-in defaults.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
With
.BR list\-checks ,
show each check's options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No findings at warning level or above.
.TP
.B 1
The most severe finding is a warning, or a check failed to run.
.TP
.B 2
At least one critical finding.
.TP
.B 3
pydebuprep itself failed: invalid arguments, an invalid or unreadable
configuration file, an unreadable baseline, an output file that could not be
written, or python3\-yaml missing when a configuration file or YAML output needs
it.
.PP
Skipped checks do not affect the exit status. A check that fails to run (for
example because a command timed out) raises it to at least 1.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is YAML with three sections.
.B report
holds defaults for
.BR format ", " output_dir ", " min_severity ", " color ", " baseline
.RB ( auto ,
.BR none ,
or a snapshot path) and
.BR snapshot_dir .
.B logs
has an
.B ignore
list of regular expressions shared by
.B journal\-errors
and
.BR dmesg\-errors ,
matched against the message text, so one pattern hides a kernel message in both.
.B checks
has one entry per check ID:
.B "enabled: false"
leaves the check out, and the other keys set that check's options. For example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
report:
  format: html
  output_dir: /var/tmp/pydebuprep
checks:
  debsums:
    enabled: false
  disk\-space:
    include_remote: true
    exclude_mounts: [/mnt/scratch*]
logs:
  ignore: ["^ACPI BIOS Error"]
.RE
.fi
.PP
Command\-line options override the file, which overrides built\-in defaults.
Relative paths in the file are relative to the file's directory. Unknown keys,
unknown checks and invalid values are errors.
.SH CHECKS
Checks run in this order and are grouped by category in the report. Use
.B list\-checks \-\-verbose
for each check's options.
.TP
.B apt\-lists\-age
(release) How stale the downloaded package lists are
.TP
.B apt\-sources
(release) Sources still on the old codename, repos disabled by the upgrade, missing distro archive, duplicates
.TP
.B os\-release
(release) Supported release; upgrade path vs. the baseline
.TP
.B apt\-autoremove
(packages) Packages \fBapt autoremove\fR would remove (simulated)
.TP
.B apt\-held
(packages) Held packages
.TP
.B apt\-upgradable
(packages) Upgrades still pending
.TP
.B config\-conflicts
(packages) \fB\(**.dpkg\-dist\fR, \fB\(**.dpkg\-new\fR, \fB\(**.ucf\-dist\fR... left in \fB/etc\fR (critical for sshd/sudoers)
.TP
.B debsums
(packages) Package files that no longer match their checksums (root, needs \fBdebsums\fR)
.TP
.B dpkg\-audit
(packages) \fBdpkg \-\-audit\fR and \fBapt\-get check\fR
.TP
.B dpkg\-rc
(packages) Removed packages with leftover config
.TP
.B obsolete\-packages
(packages) Installed packages no repository provides any more
.TP
.B boot\-space
(boot) Free space and inodes on \fB/boot\fR and the EFI partition
.TP
.B bootloader
(boot) \fBgrub.cfg\fR references the newest kernel; UEFI boot entries
.TP
.B dkms
(boot) DKMS modules built for the running kernel
.TP
.B kernel\-running
(boot) Running the newest installed kernel; reboot required
.TP
.B boot\-time
(services) \fBsystemd\-analyze\fR, the slowest units and the boot critical chain
.TP
.B cron
(services) Jobs that run programs which no longer exist; crontabs without a running cron daemon; jobs removed since the baseline
.TP
.B dmesg\-errors
(services) Kernel errors and warnings (root)
.TP
.B journal\-errors
(services) Errors in the journal since boot, grouped
.TP
.B needrestart
(services) Services still using old libraries (root, needs \fBneedrestart\fR)
.TP
.B systemd\-critical\-services
(services) Critical units (built\-in ssh, cron and journald where installed, plus configured ones) not running (failed ones are left to \fBsystemd\-failed\fR), masked, missing, disabled, or running without a required unit; units masked or missing since the baseline; listeners lost since the baseline (root)
.TP
.B systemd\-failed
(services) Failed units; services active or enabled before the upgrade that aren't now
.TP
.B systemd\-timers
(services) Enabled timers that aren't active
.TP
.B time\-sync
(services) A time synchronisation service is running and the clock is synchronised (read from the kernel, no daemon queried); service changes since the baseline
.TP
.B bind
(applications) \fBnamed\-checkconf\fR errors (root)
.TP
.B docker
(applications) Unhealthy or restarting containers; containers running before but not now. Only queried when \fBdocker.service\fR is running, so the daemon is never started (root)
.TP
.B mariadb
(applications) Data directory not upgraded to the new server version (root)
.TP
.B postfix
(applications) \fBpostconf \-n\fR warnings and errors (\fBpostfix check\fR isn't used: it creates directories)
.TP
.B postgresql
(applications) Clusters still on an older major version than the newest installed server, or left behind after migrating; clusters that should start but are down; clusters online before but not now
.TP
.B python\-venvs
(applications) Virtualenvs whose Python no longer exists, or built for an older Python
.TP
.B firewall
(network) ufw or nftables enabled but no rules loaded; rules lost since the baseline (root; only queries already\-loaded kernel modules)
.TP
.B network
(network) Default route and DNS servers; interfaces, addresses, routes, DNS and the network manager (ifupdown, netplan, NetworkManager, systemd\-networkd) compared with the baseline
.TP
.B apparmor
(security) AppArmor enabled, profiles in complain mode, denials this boot, profiles no longer enforced (root)
.TP
.B ssh\-server
(security) \fBsshd \-t\fR errors and warnings (an invalid config locks you out on restart); DSA keys in \fBauthorized_keys\fR (root)
.TP
.B unattended\-upgrades
(security) Installed but disabled, or still naming the old release in its allowed origins
.TP
.B disk\-space
(storage) Free space and inodes on local filesystems (network mounts optional); a minimum free\-space floor for \fB/\fR and \fB/var\fR
.TP
.B fstab
(storage) \fBfindmnt \-\-verify\fR; mounts that went missing since the baseline
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pydebuprep.conf
Configuration file, read if it exists. See
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/pydebuprep/examples/pydebuprep.conf
Example configuration with every default, also printed by
.BR "pydebuprep example\-config" .
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash\-completion/completions/pydebuprep
.TQ
.I /usr/share/zsh/vendor\-completions/_pydebuprep
Shell completion for bash (active when the bash\-completion package is
installed) and zsh.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pydebuprep/snapshots/
Default snapshot directory (mode 0700). Snapshots are JSON files (mode 0600)
named after the UTC time they were taken; they are private because they contain
cron command lines, network details and package lists.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PYDEBUPREP_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file to read instead of
.IR /etc/pydebuprep.conf .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colored text output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Take a snapshot, upgrade, reboot, then report:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep snapshot
sudo apt full\-upgrade   # or do\-release\-upgrade
sudo reboot
sudo pydebuprep
.RE
.fi
.PP
Write an HTML report to /var/tmp:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep report \-\-format html \-\-output\-dir /var/tmp
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check a configuration file, then report on network mounts too:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep configtest
sudo pydebuprep \-\-check\-option disk\-space.include_remote=true
.RE
.fi
.PP
Copy a report to the clipboard to paste into a Jira issue:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep \-\-format jira \-o \- | xclip \-selection clipboard
.RE
.fi
.PP
Machine\-readable output for automation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo pydebuprep report \-\-format json \-\-no\-color > report.json; echo $?
.RE
.fi
